From 821b53e22814d6451b8fd732a57f6ab8c6e7a352 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ayiejosh <132535860+ayiejosh@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 01:31:08 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] system: the zram setup that gets 732 MiB back on a 5.8 GiB
 board

Captures the swap configuration as it actually runs, not as it was intended. The board shows 5.8 GiB of
its 8 GiB, so swap is real pressure rather than a formality - but swapping to the SATA SSD is slow and
wears it. zram keeps swap pages compressed in RAM instead.

Measured on this board: 985.6 MiB of swapped data in 253.5 MiB of RAM, 3.9:1 with lz4, ~732 MiB of
usable RAM reclaimed. pgsteal_kswapd 2,932,608 and compact_stall 0 confirm it is carrying load.
An 8 GiB /swapfile stays behind it at priority -2 as the fallback.

Three pieces that matter and are easy to get wrong:
 - lz4 primary for the write path, zstd secondary only for pages that have gone cold (Nice=19 timer);
 - writeback of incompressible pages to a 2 GiB backing image, so they do not waste a full 4 KiB each;
 - hot_remove/hot_add rather than zramctl --reset, which on util-linux 2.41 hot-removes the device and
   left the board with no zram swap; the prepare step swapoffs first because hot_remove refuses while
   the block device is open.

Kernel: 6.6.98-5-aw2511, cma=256M coherent_pool=4M. Recorded in the README, with a trap worth knowing:
/proc/config.gz reports CONFIG_ZRAM_WRITEBACK, CONFIG_ZRAM_MULTI_COMP and
CONFIG_ZRAM_TRACK_ENTRY_ACTIME as NOT SET, while the shipped zram.ko.xz (vermagic matches) has all
three and live sysfs exposes them. Check sysfs or the module - and note the module is xz-compressed,
so plain strings finds nothing and looks like a missing feature.
