+## zram — compressed swap, `zram/`
+
+The board sees **5.8 GiB** of its 8 GiB. zram keeps swap pages compressed in RAM rather than
+writing them to the SSD: measured **985.6 MiB of data in 253.5 MiB of RAM (3.9:1)**, ~**732 MiB**
+of usable RAM reclaimed, with an 8 GiB `/swapfile` at priority −2 behind it as the fallback.
+
+lz4 primary for the write path, zstd secondary for pages that have gone cold, plus a 2 GiB
+writeback image for incompressible ones. Files and install steps: [`zram/`](zram/).
+
+> ⚠️ **`/proc/config.gz` lies about zram on this kernel** — it reports `CONFIG_ZRAM_WRITEBACK`,
+> `CONFIG_ZRAM_MULTI_COMP` and `CONFIG_ZRAM_TRACK_ENTRY_ACTIME` as not set, while the shipped
+> `zram.ko.xz` has all three and the live sysfs exposes them. Check sysfs or the module, and note
+> the module is xz-compressed (`plain strings` finds nothing). See [`zram/README.md`](zram/README.md).
diff --git a/system/zram/README.md b/system/zram/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..3efb7aa
--- /dev/null
+++ b/system/zram/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,82 @@
+# zram: compressed swap, tuned for a 5.8 GiB view of an 8 GiB board
+
+The A733 board shows **5.8 GiB** of its 8 GiB to Linux (~2.2 GiB is reserved for the GPU/firmware,
+plus `cma=256M`). Under a build or a browser that is tight, so swap matters — but swapping to the
+SATA SSD is both slow and wear. zram keeps swap pages **compressed in RAM** instead.
+
+## Measured effect on this board
+
+```
+985.6 MiB of swapped data  ->  253.5 MiB of RAM   (3.9 : 1, lz4)
+                            ~732 MiB of usable RAM reclaimed
+```
+
+`/proc/vmstat` confirms it is doing real work, not sitting idle: `pgsteal_kswapd 2,932,608`,
+`compact_stall 0`. Fallback layout is zram at priority **100** in front of an 8 GiB `/swapfile`
+on `/dev/sda3` at priority **−2**, so the SSD is only touched when zram fills.
+
+## How the pieces fit
+
+| file | goes to | role |
+|---|---|---|
+| `zramswap.default` | `/etc/default/zramswap` | `ALGO=lz4`, `PERCENT=100`, `PRIORITY=100` — 5.78 GiB device, 100% of RAM |
+| `zramswap.service.d/10-recomp.conf` | `/etc/systemd/system/zramswap.service.d/` | `ExecStartPre` hook — algorithm writes are only legal while `disksize=0` |
+| `zram-multicomp-prepare.sh` | `/usr/local/sbin/` | makes/recreates zram0, sets **lz4 primary + zstd secondary**, attaches writeback |
+| `zram-recompress.sh` | `/usr/local/sbin/` | ages cold pages, hands incompressible ones to writeback, recompresses the rest to zstd |
+| `zram-recompress.service` + `.timer` | `/etc/systemd/system/` | runs the above every 10 min at `Nice=19`, skipped under load ≥ 6 |
+| `zram-writeback-loop.service` | `/etc/systemd/system/` | loop-attaches `/home/radxa/zram-writeback.img` (2 GiB) before `zramswap` starts |
+
+Three design points that are not obvious:
+
+* **lz4 primary, zstd secondary.** lz4 is fast enough for the write path; zstd only ever sees pages
+  that have gone cold, so its cost competes for idle cycles rather than latency.
+* **`hot_remove`/`hot_add`, never `zramctl --reset`.** On util-linux 2.41 `--reset` hot-*removes* the
+  device and the board came back without zram swap. The prepare script also `swapoff`s first, because
+  `hot_remove` refuses while the block device is open.
+* **Writeback for incompressible pages.** A page that won't compress wastes a full 4 KiB; handing
+  those to the 2 GiB backing image is cheaper than storing them in RAM.
+
+## Install
+
+```sh
+sudo cp zramswap.default          /etc/default/zramswap
+sudo mkdir -p /etc/systemd/system/zramswap.service.d
+sudo cp zramswap.service.d/10-recomp.conf /etc/systemd/system/zramswap.service.d/
+sudo cp zram-multicomp-prepare.sh zram-recompress.sh /usr/local/sbin/
+sudo cp zram-recompress.service zram-recompress.timer zram-writeback-loop.service /etc/systemd/system/
+sudo chmod +x /usr/local/sbin/zram-multicomp-prepare.sh /usr/local/sbin/zram-recompress.sh
+truncate -s 2G /home/radxa/zram-writeback.img
+sudo systemctl daemon-reload
+sudo systemctl enable --now zram-writeback-loop zramswap zram-recompress.timer
+```
+
+## Kernel side, and one trap
+
+Running kernel: **`6.6.98-5-aw2511`** (`#5 SMP Sun Sep 20 04:35:45 UTC 2026`), arm64.
+Cmdline carries `cma=256M coherent_pool=4M`.
+
+**Do not trust `/proc/config.gz` for zram features on this kernel.** It reports:
+
+```
+# CONFIG_ZRAM_WRITEBACK is not set
+# CONFIG_ZRAM_MULTI_COMP is not set
+# CONFIG_ZRAM_TRACK_ENTRY_ACTIME is not set
+```
+
+…yet the shipped module **has all three**. Verified two independent ways:
+
+```sh
+xzcat /lib/modules/6.6.98-5-aw2511/kernel/drivers/block/zram/zram.ko.xz | strings |
+  grep -E '^(backing_dev|recomp_algorithm|idle|recompress)$'      # all four present
+ls /sys/block/zram0/{recompress,backing_dev,idle,recomp_algorithm} # all exist
+```
+
+The module (`vermagic 6.6.98-5-aw2511`, so it *is* the matching one) was built with options the
+exported config does not reflect. **Check sysfs or the module, not the config**, before concluding a
+feature is unavailable. Note the module is xz-compressed — plain `strings` on it returns nothing and
+looks like a missing feature.
+
+## What is *not* here
+
+`zswap` is not set in the config and is not used; zram is the whole strategy. THP is unavailable
+(`CONFIG_TRANSPARENT_HUGEPAGE` not set), so there is no hugepage interaction to tune.
diff --git a/system/zram/zram-multicomp-prepare.sh b/system/zram/zram-multicomp-prepare.sh
new file mode 100755
index 0000000..4dc676b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/system/zram/zram-multicomp-prepare.sh
@@ -0,0 +1,70 @@
+#!/bin/sh
+# zram-multicomp-prepare.sh — run from zramswap.service's ExecStartPre.
+#
+# It guarantees the two things zramswap itself cannot do:
+#   1. a zram device exists (the module may already be loaded with none, e.g.
+#      after any tool that removed it);
+#   2. the algorithm writes happen while disksize is 0, which is the only state
+#      the kernel accepts them in (__comp_algorithm_store -> -EBUSY afterwards).
+#
+# It never removes a device and never uses zramctl --reset: on this util-linux
+# (2.41) --reset hot-removes the device, which is what left the board without
+# zram swap after the last attempt.
+set -u
+SYS=/sys/block/zram0
+SWAPDEV=/dev/zram0
+LOG=/home/radxa/zram-multicomp/recompress.log
+log() { printf '%s [prepare] %s\n' "$(date -Is)" "$*" >> "$LOG" 2>/dev/null; }
+
+modprobe zram 2>/dev/null || true
+
+if [ ! -e "$SYS/disksize" ]; then
+  log "no zram0, adding one via zram-control"
+  cat /sys/class/zram-control/hot_add >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
+  i=0
+  while [ ! -e "$SYS/disksize" ] && [ "$i" -lt 20 ]; do sleep 0.1; i=$((i+1)); done
+fi
+[ -e "$SYS/disksize" ] || { log "could not create zram0"; exit 1; }
+
+# A device that is already initialised cannot be reconfigured: disksize_store
+# rejects writes once init_done (verified in the module source), so the only
+# legal reset is to remove the device and add a fresh one. hot_remove() calls
+# zram_reset_device() itself and refuses only while the block device is open,
+# hence swapoff first.
+if [ "$(cat "$SYS/disksize" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)" != "0" ]; then
+  if awk 'NR>1 && $1=="/dev/zram0"{f=1} END{exit !f}' /proc/swaps; then
+    swapoff "$SWAPDEV" || { log "swapoff failed"; exit 1; }
+  fi
+  i=0
+  while [ "$i" -lt 20 ]; do
+    if echo 0 > /sys/class/zram-control/hot_remove 2>/dev/null; then break; fi
+    sleep 0.2; i=$((i+1))
+  done
+  [ ! -e "$SYS/disksize" ] || { log "hot_remove did not free zram0 (still open?)"; exit 1; }
+  cat /sys/class/zram-control/hot_add >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
+  i=0
+  while [ ! -e "$SYS/disksize" ] && [ "$i" -lt 20 ]; do sleep 0.1; i=$((i+1)); done
+  [ -e "$SYS/disksize" ] || { log "hot_add did not recreate zram0"; exit 1; }
+  log "device reset via hot_remove/hot_add"
+fi
+
+printf 'lz4' > "$SYS/comp_algorithm" || { log "primary algorithm write failed"; exit 1; }
+
+# Optional writeback backing device (also only settable before init_done).
+WB_IMG=/home/radxa/zram-writeback.img
+if [ -f "$WB_IMG" ]; then
+  LOOP=$(losetup -j "$WB_IMG" 2>/dev/null | cut -d: -f1 | head -1)
+  if [ -z "$LOOP" ]; then
+    LOOP=$(losetup --find --show "$WB_IMG" 2>/dev/null)
+    log "attached $WB_IMG as $LOOP"
+  fi
+  if [ -n "$LOOP" ] && [ -e "$SYS/backing_dev" ]; then
+    echo "$LOOP" > "$SYS/backing_dev" 2>/dev/null && log "backing_dev=$LOOP" || log "backing_dev write failed"
+  fi
+fi
+if echo "algo=zstd priority=1" > "$SYS/recomp_algorithm" 2>/dev/null; then
+  log "primary lz4, secondary zstd registered"
+else
+  log "secondary algorithm write failed (continuing with lz4 only)"
+fi
+exit 0
diff --git a/system/zram/zram-recompress.service b/system/zram/zram-recompress.service
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..34d7b87
--- /dev/null
+++ b/system/zram/zram-recompress.service
@@ -0,0 +1,11 @@
+[Unit]
+Description=Recompress idle zram pages with the secondary algorithm
+After=zramswap.service
+ConditionPathExists=/sys/block/zram0/recompress
+
+[Service]
+Type=oneshot
+ExecStart=/usr/local/sbin/zram-recompress.sh
+Nice=19
+IOSchedulingClass=idle
+TimeoutStartSec=600
diff --git a/system/zram/zram-recompress.sh b/system/zram/zram-recompress.sh
new file mode 100755
index 0000000..0aa1221
--- /dev/null
+++ b/system/zram/zram-recompress.sh
@@ -0,0 +1,57 @@
+#!/bin/sh
+# zram-recompress.sh — move cold pages to the denser secondary algorithm.
+#
+# The zram device is configured with lz4 as primary (fast writes) and zstd as
+# the recompression algorithm. This script marks pages that have not been
+# touched for a while as idle, then asks zram to recompress the idle pages that
+# are still larger than `threshold` bytes. Recompressed pages stop matching the
+# threshold, so each run does less work than the previous one.
+#
+# Runs from zram-recompress.timer via zram-recompress.service with Nice=19, so
+# the compression CPU it spends competes only for otherwise idle cycles.
+set -u
+
+DEV=/sys/block/zram0
+IDLE_SECONDS=${IDLE_SECONDS:-1800}
+THRESHOLD=${THRESHOLD:-1500}
+MAX_LOAD=${MAX_LOAD:-6}
+
+LOG=/home/radxa/zram-multicomp/recompress.log
+log() { printf '%s [zram-recompress] %s\n' "$(date -Is)" "$*" | tee -a "$LOG" 2>/dev/null; }
+
+# Stock module (or no device): nothing to do.
+[ -e "$DEV/recompress" ] || exit 0
+[ -e "$DEV/disksize" ] || exit 0
+[ "$(cat "$DEV/disksize" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)" -gt 0 ] || exit 0
+
+# The secondary algorithm can only be registered before the device is
+# initialised (the kernel returns -EBUSY afterwards), so it is set by
+# zramswap.service's ExecStartPre drop-in, not here. Warn if it is missing.
+if ! grep -q 'zstd' "$DEV/recomp_algorithm" 2>/dev/null; then
+  log "no secondary algorithm configured - check zramswap.service.d/10-recomp.conf"
+  exit 0
+fi
+
+# Skip when something else is already working the CPU.
+LOAD=$(awk '{print int($1)}' /proc/loadavg)
+[ "$LOAD" -ge "$MAX_LOAD" ] && { log "load $LOAD too high, skipping"; exit 0; }
+
+before_data=$(awk '{print $1}' "$DEV/mm_stat")
+before_compr=$(awk '{print $2}' "$DEV/mm_stat")
+
+# Incompressible pages waste a full 4 KB each; if a backing device is
+# configured, hand them to it before spending CPU on recompression.
+if [ -e "$DEV/backing_dev" ] && [ -n "$(cat "$DEV/backing_dev" 2>/dev/null)" ]; then
+  echo huge > "$DEV/writeback" 2>/dev/null || log "huge writeback failed"
+fi
+
+echo "$IDLE_SECONDS" > "$DEV/idle" 2>/dev/null || log "idle write failed (no access-time tracking?)"
+if ! echo "type=idle threshold=$THRESHOLD" > "$DEV/recompress" 2>/dev/null; then
+  log "recompress write failed"
+  exit 0
+fi
+
+after_data=$(awk '{print $1}' "$DEV/mm_stat")
+after_compr=$(awk '{print $2}' "$DEV/mm_stat")
+log "idle>=${IDLE_SECONDS}s, threshold=${THRESHOLD}B: data $((${after_data:-0}/1048576))MB compr $((${after_compr:-0}/1048576))MB" \
+    "(was $((${before_data:-0}/1048576))MB / $((${before_compr:-0}/1048576))MB)"
diff --git a/system/zram/zram-recompress.timer b/system/zram/zram-recompress.timer
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..7d130e6
--- /dev/null
+++ b/system/zram/zram-recompress.timer
@@ -0,0 +1,10 @@
+[Unit]
+Description=Periodic idempotent-aging recompression of zram pages
+
+[Timer]
+OnBootSec=2min
+OnUnitActiveSec=10min
+AccuracySec=1min
+
+[Install]
